Replace the command hold design with carry-over

Three adversarial reviews of the proposed per-voice hold found that
holding commands behind loads is the wrong mechanism:

- a load can hang (an uncached HTTP play opens with no connect or
  response timeout), and a held voice_stop or fader move would then
  wait forever, while the sound already playing kept going;
- holding per voice made unrelated commands wait in chains, a global
  waiting cap turned one stuck voice into failures everywhere, and a
  release burst could overflow the audio command ring and drop the
  stopall that triggered it;
- ordering plays within a voice bought nothing and cost latency.

docs/command-ordering.md now describes the approved replacement: a
command applies at once to the sounds already playing and carries its
setting (speed, volume, seek, voice_volume) onto every matching play
that is still loading, which starts with it; stop, voice_stop and
voice_fade_out cancel matching unstarted plays, as stopall and fadeall
already cancel every one; nothing waits. It lists where the result still
differs from one-at-a-time processing (timing, stops cancelling instead
of interrupting, voice_volume onto a voice that does not exist yet, HTTP
plays whose client gave up, windowed plays, crossfades across voices).

docs/bugs.md gains the pre-existing issues the reviews surfaced and that
were checked against the code: windowed plays ignore start_position_ms;
volume and seek do not reach streamed sources; a shutdown signal can be
missed while a command is handled (tokio drops a signal broadcast that
finds no listener, and the loop recreates its listeners each iteration);
a huge start_position_ms or seek overflows in debug builds.

No code changes.

Prompts that led to this work:

- "Let's go with the per-voice hold fix. Make sure to think through all
  the possible implications ... Consider spawning adversarial review
  agents both before starting the fix and after implementing it."
- Decisions after the reviews: carry settings over instead of holding
  commands; stops and fades cancel matching unstarted plays.

